 `bun check` runs the project's `check` script; a script that invokes a TypeScript checker uses that checker's sidecar resolution.
 
 - **Content.** Declarations are exactly what idiomatic Effect TypeScript would declare. Ordinary interface and object-type properties, including optional members and index signatures, keep exactly the source's written `readonly` modifiers, including in nested object types; class fields keep their written modifiers too. Each keeps its source form: a function declaration is a `declare function`, and a `const` holding an arrow is a `declare const`. A `service`, `error`, `schema`, `schema error` or `data` declaration is declared as TypeScript's declaration emit of its Effect TypeScript counterpart, which introduces a `Name_base` constant for a class-call heritage such as `Context.Service<…>()("k")`. The output imports every name the source had in scope without an import: each root module as `import type { Effect, Stream } from "effect"`, each subpath namespace from its subpath, and a module whose bare name is a JavaScript global's (`Array`, `Console`) under a fresh alias, as emitted code does ([Services](005-services.md#effects-modules-are-in-scope)). A declaration writes such a name only where the source writes it: an inferred signature that a library declares with a name the file never writes keeps TypeScript's import type, so `Schema.decodeUnknownSync(Schema.String)` declares `options?: import("effect/SchemaAST").ParseOptions`. A form that stands for a module writes its name: an Effect function, `A!E!R`, `try` or a bare Effect global such as `sleep` writes `Effect`, which its Effect TypeScript imports. Nothing in it depends on `vibe.effectModules` or `vibe.effectGlobals`. The generic-`data` helper for `data Outcome<A>` is named `$Outcome$Definition`; if the module already declares that name, the compiler appends `$2`, `$3`, … and takes the first free name, so the name is the same on every build (VIP-28, amended). An `error` or `schema error` keeps its static `[Symbol.customMatcher]` member, and a declaration file that declares one begins with `/// <reference types="vibelang-runtime" />`, which declares the `Symbol.customMatcher` type, so plain `tsc` checks it and matching across packages works at run time (VIP-27, amended).
-- **Specifiers.** A relative import of a VibeLang module, `./user.vibe`, is declared through its `.js` output, `./user.js`, by default: no TypeScript resolves `./user.vibe` to `user.d.ts` (D298). A package whose sources spell relative imports another way keeps that spelling with the project setting `vibelang.declarationImportStyle`: `"extensionless"` declares `./user`, `".ts"` declares `./user.ts`, and `".js"` is the default. Under either of the other two, every other specifier stays as the source wrote it, `./plain.ts` included, as `tsc` keeps it in the declaration of a `.ts` file. Any other value is a configuration error. An import the declaration file adds, such as `import { Effect } from "effect"`, takes the quotes of the file's first written import or export specifier, as an import the TypeScript language service adds does; a file with none gets double quotes.
+- **Specifiers.** A relative import of a VibeLang module, `./user.vibe`, is declared through its `.js` output, `./user.js`, by default: no TypeScript resolves `./user.vibe` to `user.d.ts` (D298). A package whose sources spell relative imports another way keeps that spelling with the project setting `vibelang.declarationImportStyle`: `"extensionless"` declares `./user`, `".ts"` declares `./user.ts`, and `".js"` is the default. Under either of the other two, every other specifier stays as the source wrote it, `./plain.ts` included, as `tsc` keeps it in the declaration of a `.ts` file. The setting spells an import type the compiler writes itself the same way, `import("./user.ts").User` for a type inferred from `user.vibe`, in the declaration of a `.vibe` or a `.ts` file. Any other value is a configuration error. An import the declaration file adds, such as `import { Effect } from "effect"`, takes the quotes of the file's first written import or export specifier, as an import the TypeScript language service adds does; a file with none gets double quotes. Module types a file writes through one of its own imports, `HashSet<string>` through `import { HashSet, Option } from 'effect'`, stay in one import, in the written order and quotes, and an added name joins it: `import { Effect, HashSet, Option } from 'effect'`.
 - **Producer environments.** An inferred producer declaration exposes the environment `Exclude<RBody, Scope.Scope>` for its body requirements `RBody`, because the accepted `Stream.callback` provides `Scope`. Preserve other services, ordinary checks on explicit annotations and values of type `Scope.Scope` ([Producers](002-running.md#producers)).
 - **Thrown types.** Each declaration carries its thrown type as [Thrown types](003-failures.md#thrown-types) defines it. An effectful function carries no `@throws`. Every other call signature the output emits carries `@throws {T}`, `@throws {never}` included, so no reader depends on a default: functions, methods, constructors, callable `const`s, `async` functions, and each call, construct or method signature of an exported `interface`, type literal or `type` alias (VIP-4, amended). A bare function type (`() => string`) stays a function type. Getters and setters carry none.
 - **Each call signature carries its own contract** ([Thrown types](003-failures.md#thrown-types), VIP-4, amended): every overload, and a returned callable's own signature. TypeScript attaches JSDoc to a call signature but not to a function type, so a callable `const` is declared with a type literal whose call signature carries the tag. A written `@throws {T}` is a checked upper bound, so the output copies `T`; otherwise it writes the inferred type. A tag on an alias of a function type does not attach to its call signature; write `type Reader = { /** @throws {E} */ (): string }` instead (VIP-4, amended). A foreign callable binding without a tag on itself or its declared call signature throws `unknown`, whether imported from source or an emitted `.d.ts`, even with a written function type (VIP-4, amended).
